Q: Calendar: adding a day time the button is clicked [Java] A: <p>the question was asked before!
do your research instead of just posting a question!
this is your answer:</p>

<pre><code>public class DateUtil
{
    public static Date addDays(Date date, int days)
    {
        Calendar cal = Calendar.getInstance();
        cal.setTime(date);
        cal.add(Calendar.DATE, days); //minus number would decrement the days
        return cal.getTime();
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>To add one day, per click, call it like this:</p>

<pre><code>String sourceDate = "2012-02-29";
SimpleDateFormat format = new SimpleDateFormat("yyyy-MM-dd");
Date myDate = format.parse(sourceDate);
myDate = DateUtil.addDays(myDate, 1);
</code></pre>

Q: Slider value twitches when thumb button is released (iOS 26) A: <p>I found a possible workaround.</p>
<p>A simultaneous <code>DragGesture</code> can be used to detect, when the thumb button is being dragged. Updates to the state variable can then be suppressed if the drag has already ended. One way to intercept the updates is to use a computed binding.</p>
<p>The key changes are as follows:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>@State private var value = CGFloat.zero
@State private var isEditing = false
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>private var sliderVal: Binding&lt;CGFloat&gt; {
    .init(
        get: { value },
        set: { newValue in
            if isEditing {
                value = newValue
            }
        }
    )
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>Slider(value: sliderVal, in: 0...300, step: 1)
    .simultaneousGesture(
        DragGesture(minimumDistance: 0)
            .onChanged { val in
                isEditing = true
            }
            .onEnded { val in
                isEditing = false
            }
    )
</code></pre>
<p>You might expect that the value of the flag <code>isEditing</code> would correspond to the value that the <code>Slider</code> delivers in its callback function <code>onEditingChanged</code>. Unfortunately, this is not the case. The <code>Slider</code> only calls through to the function with a value of <code>false</code> when the thumb button has gone back to its idle appearance.</p>
<hr />
<p>This code adds quite a lot of weight to the original example. However, it can be implemented as a generic wrapper for a <code>Slider</code>, as follows:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>struct BufferedSlider&lt;V&gt;: View
where V: BinaryFloatingPoint, V.Stride: BinaryFloatingPoint {
    @Binding private var value: V
    private let bounds: ClosedRange&lt;V&gt;
    private let step: V.Stride
    private let onEditingChanged: (Bool) -&gt; Void
    @State private var isEditing = false

    init(
        value: Binding&lt;V&gt;,
        in bounds: ClosedRange&lt;V&gt; = 0...1,
        step: V.Stride = 1,
        onEditingChanged: @escaping (Bool) -&gt; Void = { _ in }
    ) {
        self._value = value
        self.bounds = bounds
        self.step = step
        self.onEditingChanged = onEditingChanged
        self.isEditing = isEditing
    }

    private var sliderVal: Binding&lt;V&gt; {
        .init(
            get: { value },
            set: { newValue in
                if isEditing {
                    value = newValue
                }
            }
        )
    }

    var body: some View {
        Slider(value: sliderVal, in: bounds, step: step)
            .simultaneousGesture(
                DragGesture(minimumDistance: 0)
                    .onChanged { val in
                        isEditing = true
                    }
                    .onEnded { val in
                        isEditing = false
                    }
            )
            .onChange(of: isEditing) { _, newVal in
                onEditingChanged(newVal)
            }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Now, the only change needed in the original example is to replace <code>Slider</code> with <code>BufferedSlider</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>BufferedSlider(value: $sliderVal, in: 0...300, step: 1)
    .padding(.horizontal)
</code></pre>

Q: Bold box style in ncurses A: <p>As &quot;too large&quot; I understand wider than <code>char</code>. To use unicode chars with ncurses you need a special version of the library and you need to link with <code>-lncursesw</code>.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;locale.h&gt;
#include &lt;ncurses.h&gt;

void draw_double_border(WINDOW *win) {
    // Define the wide characters for the border
    cchar_t ls, rs, ts, bs, tl, tr, bl, br;

    setcchar(&amp;ls, L&quot;║&quot;, ALL_INDICATIONS, 0, NULL); // Left side
    setcchar(&amp;rs, L&quot;║&quot;, ALL_INDICATIONS, 0, NULL); // Right side
    setcchar(&amp;ts, L&quot;═&quot;, ALL_INDICATIONS, 0, NULL); // Top side
    setcchar(&amp;bs, L&quot;═&quot;, ALL_INDICATIONS, 0, NULL); // Bottom side
    setcchar(&amp;tl, L&quot;╔&quot;, ALL_INDICATIONS, 0, NULL); // Top left
    setcchar(&amp;tr, L&quot;╗&quot;, ALL_INDICATIONS, 0, NULL); // Top right
    setcchar(&amp;bl, L&quot;╚&quot;, ALL_INDICATIONS, 0, NULL); // Bottom left
    setcchar(&amp;br, L&quot;╝&quot;, ALL_INDICATIONS, 0, NULL); // Bottom right

    // Apply the border to the window
    wborder_set(win, &amp;ls, &amp;rs, &amp;ts, &amp;bs, &amp;tl, &amp;tr, &amp;bl, &amp;br);
}
</code></pre>
